0912.2625	future_work	Having shown that \(\mathcal{P}(\mathbb{N})\) is a central assumption in Theorem REF , we now turn to the question whether homogeneous sets of lower complexity can be found.	Having shown that \(\mathcal{P}(\mathbb{N})\) is a central assumption in Theorem REF , we now turn to the question whether homogeneous sets of lower complexity can be found. Let \(\mathcal{L}\) denote the regular \(\omega\)-language \(L\). Furthermore, \(\mathcal{F}\) comprises all 2-sets \(F\) such that \(\operatorname{supp}(F)\) is finite or \(\ldots\) . The set \(\mathcal{C}\) is the complement of \(\mathcal{D}\) in \(\mathcal{E}\) . This completes the construction of the \(\omega\)-partition \(\ldots\) . Note that \(\ldots\) is an injective \(\omega\)-automatic presentation of \(\ldots\) . By Theorem REF , \(\ldots\) has an \(\omega\)- or an \(\omega^2\)-homogeneous set of size \(\ldots\) . We convince ourselves that \(\ldots\) has large homogeneous sets of both types.	0.9
