with rate {{formula:906cc542-fc3c-40cd-bd3f-b073d464dffa}} . Also the waiting time at the source satisfies: {{formula:10af08c1-a85d-4552-8189-e7d191fb9d9b}} A well-known result is that (see, e.g. {{cite:bbc66dc6472dcacfa9b148d8ae7c1f80aedef85f}}), if the arrival and service processes satisfy the stability condition, then the Lindley's recursion has the solution: {{formula:ba97abd6-72e4-48cd-a275-0da17881e2da}} where the partial sum {{formula:b318dd0e-59ca-4fea-8a13-7b9863226c5a}} and {{formula:b2efb31f-50a7-464a-8fae-51d81c1f0c96}} . Hence {{formula:fdf762d7-c5b6-4828-b007-3f4ce9d8fed6}} From (REF ) we have {{formula:609a02a6-041d-4adc-a770-5d856d5751e3}} for {{formula:348d249d-2f55-4b3d-9156-6618e30e0713}} , and 0 otherwise. Plug this into (REF ) we have {{formula:1cfdf722-7352-4a56-a449-551bb44c8823}} Hence the recursive relation if we move {{formula:13b2de95-b55a-4ee0-b0fc-9a96caa7b098}} to the left-hand-side: {{formula:a5ecdcfc-8942-4f89-94f9-3a4506415ebf}} Now from (REF ) we have {{formula:2c103b55-c730-4811-8cd2-da80ef271e36}} . Plug this in the above (REF ) we have {{formula:85936347-3951-4559-868e-555b67240bac}} Do this inductively, we have {{formula:bdca256a-5d2f-4a72-8b6a-42ead1c46851}} If we also add {{formula:98dc1450-dac9-4723-8be8-94d5ab99ee85}} to the above equation, after rearranging terms we have: {{formula:a00a213f-532e-41a6-8b48-1da57fb18f60}} Note that {{formula:2f5bce44-5119-4e8e-82f4-ef4a7bf3f491}} is independent of {{formula:f1f09ff4-cc32-49f7-b52d-0f7a64bbaaf7}} . For long queue we can ignored the last four terms caused by edge effect (the source and end queue of the multihop relay network). By stationarity of the service process {{formula:71cc014c-4565-41ff-8cbb-c7a8494057c2}} has the same distribution as {{formula:22662790-abad-43b0-b39b-b2e5645f9a54}} . Then (REF ) reduces to the case studied in {{cite:bbc66dc6472dcacfa9b148d8ae7c1f80aedef85f}} and we can borrow the large deviation argument therein to derive the exponent {{formula:7f012ca9